The '''1977 Baliscan premiership election''' was held on 4 December 1977 to elect the [[Secretary General of Balisca]]. It was the first premiership election to take place after the onset of the [[Special Period]], and followed the [[1977 Baliscan confederal election]]. [[Ecological Action|Ecologist]] candidate [[Otávia Brito]] won the election in a landslide.

A total of 43 million people voted in the election, reaching a voter turnout of 83.09%.

== Background ==

{{main|1977 Baliscan confederal election|October 76|Special Period}}

The premiership election took place against the backdrop of the [[Special Period]]—the political and economic crisis that followed the [[October 76]] crisis and the [[Camboriú War]]. The [[1977 Baliscan confederal election|confederal election]] of 9–16 October 1977 had resulted in a catastrophic defeat for the governing [[Revolutionary Front]] (FR), which collapsed from 312 seats to 36 in the [[Confederal Congress]]. The anti-authoritarian bloc—uniting under the [[Popular Assemblies Movement]] (including [[Ecological Action]], the [[Cosmopolitan Collective]], and the [[Indigenous Sovereignty Alliance]])—won a commanding majority.

Under the Baliscan constitution, the all elections, including those for the premiership are elected by [[W:Instant-runoff voting|instant-runoff voting]] in a direct national ballot. While confederal elections determine the composition of the Congress, the premiership election determines who leads the confederal government. The two elections were separated by approximately six weeks to allow the new Congress to convene and for the premiership campaign to proceed with the political landscape clarified.

The incumbent Secretary General, [[Mário Sá Justino]], had been [[W:Censure|censured]] by the Confederal Congress on 28 December 1976 for his imposition of emergency measures during the October 76 crisis. Although Justino remained in office in a caretaker capacity pending the election, his authority was effectively destroyed. The Revolutionary Front, shattered by the confederal election result, would unite with other sympathetic platforms under the banner of the [[Front for National Unity (Balisca)|Front for National Unity]] (FpUN) for the premiership contest.

== Candidates ==

=== Otávia Brito — Ecological Action ===

{{main|Otávia Brito|Ecological Action}}

[[Otávia Brito]], an environmental engineer and [[Ecological Action]] delegate, had emerged as the most prominent voice of the anti-authoritarian movement during the October 76 crisis. She had participated in the student protests in [[Castejón]] and helped organize the solidarity network that sustained the [[general strike]]. Her nomination as the Ecological Action candidate for Secretary General was confirmed at the platform's convention on 5 November 1977.

Brito's candidacy was supported by the broader anti-authoritarian coalition, the [[Popular Assemblies Movement]], and by platforms the [[Indigenous Sovereignty Alliance]], and the [[Cosmopolitan Collective]]. While each of these platforms fielded their own candidates in the first round, all endorsed Brito as their second preference and committed to supporting her in the event of a runoff. Her campaign emphasized ecological governance, a negotiated end to the [[Camboriú War]], [[Low Impact Initiative|decarbonization]] in the face of [[1980s oil crisis|the oil crisis]], and the full restoration of civil liberties.

Brito's identity as an openly lesbian women was noted internationally, though within Balisca it was treated as unremarkable by most commentators. Her candidacy symbolized the broader social liberation that the anti-authoritarian movement represented, contrasting with the more left-conservative social policies of the Revolutionary Front administration.

=== Tomás Echeverría — Frente por la Defensa Nacional ===

{{main|Tomás Echeverría|Front for National Unity}}

[[Tomás Echeverría]] was a moderate figure within the [[Revolutionary Front (Balisca)|Revolutionary Front]] who had not been directly implicated in the October 76 crisis. A former minister of infrastructure and a veteran of the [[Baliscan Revolution]], Riera was selected as the candidate of the [[Front for National Unity]] (FpUN)—a reorganized coalition of the Revolutionary Front and its remaining allies—on 12 November 1977.

Echeverría's campaign emphasized continuity in the conduct of the [[Camboriú War]] and [[Bight of Ayorin Crisis]], arguing that a change in leadership during an active conflict would endanger national security. He defended the nuclear program as essential to deterrence against [[Potocsí]] and warned that Brito's anti-nuclear position would potentially leave Balisca vulnerable. Echeverría attempted to distance himself from Justino's authoritarian measures while maintaining that the FR's governance had been necessary during the crisis.

=== Other candidates ===

In addition to Brito and Echeverría, several other candidates contested the first round:

* '''[[Jordi Puigvert]]''' — [[Democratic Renewal Platform]]: The centrist-reformist candidate positioned himself as a moderate alternative. Puigvert's campaign emphasized institutional reform and economic modernization.
* '''[[Ñanderú Kuarahy]]''' — [[Indigenous Sovereignty Alliance]]: The indigenous rights candidate campaigned on expanded self-governance, territorial rights, and cultural preservation.
* '''[[Amélia Carvalho]]''' — [[Cosmopolitan Collective]]: The CC candidate emphasized participatory democracy, the end of emergency measures, and grassroots governance. Carvalho's candidacy served primarily to mobilize the Cosmopolite base, with second preferences directed to Brito.
* [[Vicente Riera|'''Vicente Riera''']] — [[Confederal Labor Alliance]]: A minor labor-oriented platform candidate.

All eliminated candidates endorsed Brito as their second preference, with the exception of Riera, who declined to make a recommendation.

== Campaign ==

The premiership campaign, which formally began after the confederal election results were certified on 20 October 1977, lasted approximately five weeks. The campaign was dominated by three issues: the [[Camboriú War]], the [[Balisca and weapons of mass destruction|nuclear program]], and the legacy of [[October 76]].

